GOAL: fix the bug in `objToLocalTS` in /luxon/src/impl/util.js (lines ~155-172).

CURRENT (buggy) CODE:

  export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
    let d = Date.UTC(
      obj.year,
      obj.month - 1,
      obj.day,
      obj.hour,
      obj.minute,
      obj.second,
      obj.millisecond
    );

    // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
    if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
      d = new Date(d);
      d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
    }
    return +d;
  }

The bug: `d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900)` only replaces the year, keeping the month/day that were already normalized in the 19XX/2000 space. When a day overflow lands on Feb 29 of 2000 (a leap year) the year is then forced to 100 (not a leap year), so the result is off by a day; e.g. `DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ days: 61 })` yields 0100-03-01 instead of the correct 0100-03-02. Conversely year 0 is a leap year but 1900 is not.

FIX: replace the body of the `if` block with a single call that re-sets the year, month and day together, so the raw fields are normalized in the target year, and add an explanatory comment. The resulting function must be exactly:

  export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
    let d = Date.UTC(
      obj.year,
      obj.month - 1,
      obj.day,
      obj.hour,
      obj.minute,
      obj.second,
      obj.millisecond
    );

    // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
    if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
      d = new Date(d);
      // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
      // so if we set the year first, we could end up with a date like Feb 29 2000, which is invalid in year 100
      d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
    }
    return +d;
  }

Do not change anything else in the repo.

